<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Paretto.Domain\Paretto.Domain.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Block 6 (Login + esquema de autenticación por sesión): SessionAuthenticationHandler needs
    `AuthenticationHandler<TOptions>` / `AuthenticationSchemeOptions` (namespace
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication). The spec's package table (Block 1) names
    `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.Abstractions` as the NuGet package for this — verified
    during this block that the package is frozen at 2.3.12 (nuget.org), the exact same situation
    Block 4 already documented for `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity` vs.
    `Microsoft.Extensions.Identity.Core`: it stopped shipping as a standalone package once
    ASP.NET Core 3.0 moved these APIs into the shared framework. A `FrameworkReference` to
    `Microsoft.AspNetCore.App` is the framework-recommended way for a class library (non-Web SDK)
    to consume ASP.NET Core APIs without pulling a legacy/frozen NuGet package — same intent as the
    spec's justification (implement a custom `AuthenticationHandler<TOptions>`), technically
    corrected the same way Block 4 corrected the Identity package choice.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="10.0.11">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Tools" Version="10.0.11">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Block 2 (Storage: almacenamiento de fotos): AzureBlobStorageService needs BlobServiceClient /
    BlobContainerClient / BlobSasBuilder. 12.29.1 is the latest stable release on nuget.org (not a
    beta), the first version whose dependency graph explicitly targets net10.0, and was verified
    with "dotnet list package" in vulnerable mode (see report) to have no known Critical/High
    advisories.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Azure.Storage.Blobs" Version="12.29.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Block 3 (Infrastructure: validación NSFW): NsfwSpyContentScanner wraps NsfwSpyNS.NsfwSpy
    (ML.NET-based nudity/pornography classifier). 3.5.0 is the latest stable release on nuget.org
    (not a beta). "dotnet list package vulnerable" flagged its transitive dependency
    Magick.NET-Q16-AnyCPU 11.1.2 with multiple High-severity advisories — pinned explicitly to
    14.16.0 (latest stable) below to override that transitive version. Re-verified clean after the
    pin (see report).

    FIX-004 correction (docs/daw/specs/rca-FIX-004.md): the note above used to say "NsfwSpy only
    calls the stable MagickImage(byte[]) constructor and ToByteArray(MagickFormat), unaffected by
    the major-version bump" — that was wrong. NsfwSpy's WebP branch calls
    ToByteArray((MagickFormat)179), a raw integer compiled against 11.1.2's enum layout (ordinal
    179 = Png there). 14.16.0 inserted ~12 new MagickFormat members before that position, so the
    same integer now resolves to Phm (Portable HalfFloat Map) — NsfwSpy silently reencodes every
    WebP to the wrong format and classification always fails. The real mitigation is
    NsfwSpyClassifier reencoding WebP to PNG itself, by the enum's NAME
    (MagickFormat.Png), before NsfwSpy's own broken branch ever runs — not the version pin alone.

    CODE closeout (FEAT-001b, Block 8, SAST F-SAST-13): same situation for a second transitive
    dependency — NsfwSpy → Microsoft.ML → Newtonsoft.Json 10.0.3, High severity
    (GHSA-5crp-9r3c-p9vr, resource-exhaustion via deeply nested JSON, fixed in 13.0.1). Pinned
    explicitly to 13.0.4 (latest stable) below, same override pattern as Magick.NET-Q16-AnyCPU.
    Nothing in this codebase calls Newtonsoft.Json directly — the pin only overrides the transitive
    resolution MimeTypesMap gets at build time. Re-verified clean after the pin (see report).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NsfwSpy" Version="3.5.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Magick.NET-Q16-AnyCPU" Version="14.16.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
